# LIVE PIN (todo/t0953) — the VECTOR arm of the builtin-HOF closure-argument
# env drain, and the UNBOUNDEDNESS cell that motivated it.
#
# ⭐ GRADUATED R50 Track E. This was a KNOWN GAP with an `#[ignore]`d test; the
# Vector-HOF expander now drains the temp, the fixture is ASan-CLEAN, and the
# `#[ignore]` is dropped IN PLACE. The file stays in `known_gaps/` because
# `assert_gg_sanitize_clean` takes a subdirectory stem and the directory is
# `OUT` of both the sanitize corpus and `runtime_parity_corpus` — a live gate
# at no corpus cost. `todo/t0953` STAYS OPEN on its plain-call cell (cell B).
#
# ⚠ THE 20-TRIP LOOP IS LOAD-BEARING. It is the only row in Track E's pin set
# that can see a drain hoisted from per-CALL to per-FUNCTION: under a shared
# per-function ledger an N-trip loop leaks N-1 records and a one-trip fixture
# stays green. The pre-fix measurement below is what proves one mint per trip.
#
# `todo/t0953` owns the class and proves the WRITE SITE with a negative
# control: a plain user function taking a `Callable` leaks the same frame with
# no builtin HOF in the program, and the same program with a NAMED callee
# leaks nothing. What its repro does not show — because two calls cannot show
# it — is that the leak is UNBOUNDED PER CALL rather than a fixed cost.
#
# This one does: twenty calls, twenty records. Measured 2026-09-03,
# `gg build --sanitize` + `ASAN_OPTIONS=detect_leaks=1:exitcode=0
# LSAN_OPTIONS=use_stacks=0` — `160 byte(s) leaked in 20 allocation(s)`, all
# `__gorget_closure_env_alloc`. Change the trip count and the record count
# follows it. That is the property that makes the class a resource bug in a
# loop rather than a constant overhead, and it is what the allowlist's
# big-groups table means by "UNBOUNDED per call".
#
# HEAP-FORCED: the env is a real `malloc`, so LeakSanitizer sees it under the
# sweep's own conservative-root setting.
#
# NOT A SECOND FILING: `todo/t0953` owns this: this is one more cell of its
# evidence, cited from it — the UNBOUNDEDNESS axis its own two-call probes
# cannot reach.
#
# INTENDED, AND NOW ASSERTED LIVE: prints `120` and runs ASan-CLEAN. The stdout
# was always correct — the LEAK was the gap, so this repro means nothing outside
# the sanitizer, which is why it is wired to `assert_gg_sanitize_clean` and
# never to `run_gg`.

void main():
    Vector[int] xs = [1, 2]
    int total = 0
    int i = 0
    while i < 20:
        Vector[int] doubled = xs.map((int x): x * 2)
        for d in doubled:
            total = total + d
        i = i + 1
    print(total)
